<Docs slug="select-a-photo">
  <p>
    LightPhotos has two views of the same folder. The grid is for deciding
    which photos matter; the Loupe is for working on one of them. Double-click
    a thumbnail to open it in the Loupe, and click the arrow at the left of the
    title bar to go back. Nothing is lost going back and forth &mdash; the
    grid and the Loupe are two windows onto one set of files.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The rest of the folder stays in reach.</strong> A filmstrip runs
      along the bottom of the Loupe, so you can move through a burst at full
      size without going back to the grid.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fit to judge, 1:1 to check.</strong> A photo opens fitted to the
      window, which is how you judge composition and color. Focus and noise
      only show at 100%, so zoom in before you decide a frame is sharp. Scroll
      over the photo to zoom at the pointer, or use the zoom control in the
      bar beneath it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Compare against the original.</strong> View &rarr;
      <strong>Before / After</strong> splits the photo into the original and
      your edit, side by side &mdash; the quickest way to see if you have
      gone too far.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    When several frames are nearly the same, open them together instead:
    <a href="/docs/compare-a-stack.html">compare a stack</a>. Once you have
    the photo you want, the panel on the right is where you
    <a href="/docs/adjust.html">adjust the settings</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
